{"packet_id":"D067-01","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Security deposit cap of one and one-half months' rent","requirement":"A landlord may not collect a security deposit of more than one and one-half times one month's rent, and any yearly increase in the deposit may not exceed 10% of the current deposit. Prepaid rent held as security counts toward the cap.","key_value":"1.5 months' rent; yearly increase max 10%","coverage_conditions":"All residential rentals in New Jersey where the landlord collects a security deposit; collecting a deposit is optional and it can be less than the maximum.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"all residential rentals in New Jersey"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-21.2","quoted_span":"The maximum-security deposit to be collected by the landlord cannot be more than one and one-half times one month’s rent ( N.J.S.A. 46:8-21.2 ). It can be less. Any additional yearly security deposit increase may not exceed 10% of the current security deposit.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-01","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Security deposit account and interest requirements","requirement":"A landlord who receives security deposit money must hold it in an insured New Jersey interest-bearing account, or for 10 or more units a New Jersey money market fund; the interest or earnings belong to the tenant and are paid on the lease renewal or anniversary, or on January 31.","key_value":"10 units; interest-bearing account","coverage_conditions":"Landlords or designees who receive security deposit money; separate account rules for ten or fewer units and ten or more units.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"landlords holding security deposits; ten-unit threshold for account type"},"exemptions":"Security deposits for seasonal use or rental, meaning use for not more than 125 consecutive days by a person with a permanent residence elsewhere.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-19","quoted_span":"A landlord or designee who receives security deposit money for ten or less units must deposit that money in an insured bank or savings and loan association located in New Jersey in an interest-bearing account at the current interest rate at the time of deposit.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-01","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Security deposit account notice and tenant rent-credit remedy","requirement":"The landlord must notify the tenant in writing of the bank, deposit amount, account type and interest rate within 30 days of receiving the deposit and at each annual interest payment. Failure lets the tenant credit the deposit plus 7% per annum to rent.","key_value":"30-day notice; 7% per annum","coverage_conditions":"Tenants whose landlords hold their security deposit and fail to give the annual notice or pay the annual interest.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"all residential security deposits"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Tenant may apply the security deposit plus 7% per annum interest to rent; the landlord may not then demand an additional security deposit.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-19.1","quoted_span":"If a tenant does not receive proper notice or is not paid interest as required, the tenant may use the security deposit for payment of rent by giving the landlord written notice that the security money plus interest at the rate of 7% per annum be applied to rent payments or payments due or to become due from the tenant. However, if the tenant does not receive the annual notice at the time of the annual interest payment, or is not paid the annual interest, as required, the tenant must give the landlord written notice and allow the landlord 30 days to comply with the annual interest payment and notice requirements.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-01","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Security deposit return within 30 days and double damages","requirement":"Within 30 days after a tenancy ends the landlord must return the deposit plus interest less any itemized deductions; a landlord who fails to do so may be liable for double the amount wrongly withheld, plus costs and attorney's fees.","key_value":"30 days; double damages","coverage_conditions":"All residential tenancies in New Jersey; if a tenant breaks the lease without legal cause, the 30 days runs from re-renting or lease expiry, whichever is first.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"all residential tenancies"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Tenant may sue for double the amount wrongfully withheld; if successful the court may award double the amount plus court costs and reasonable attorney's fees.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-21.1","quoted_span":"Within 30 days after the termination of a tenancy, a landlord must return the security deposit, plus interest earned less deductions, to the tenant ( N.J.S.A. 46:8-21.1). Deductions may include the cost of any damages over and above normal wear and tear, and any other money due the landlord under the terms of the lease.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-01","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Security deposit return after displacement by fire, flood or condemnation","requirement":"When a tenant is displaced by fire, flood, condemnation or evacuation, the landlord must return the security deposit within five business days and tell the tenant within three business days where to collect it.","key_value":"5 business days","coverage_conditions":"Tenants displaced by fire, flood, condemnation or evacuation where a public official has posted a no-occupancy notice or certified displacement will last more than seven days.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"displaced residential tenants"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-21.1","quoted_span":"Within five (5) business days after a tenant is displaced by fire, flood, condemnation, or evacuation, the landlord must return the security deposit. The law requires the return when either an authorized public official has posted a notice prohibiting occupancy or has certified that the displacement is expected to continue longer than seven (7) days.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-01","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Security deposit return after domestic violence lease termination","requirement":"A tenant who terminates a lease under the Safe Housing Act is entitled to the return of the security deposit; the landlord must make the deposit and interest available within 15 business days and notify the tenant within three business days where to collect it.","key_value":"15 business days","coverage_conditions":"Tenants who terminate a residential lease under the Safe Housing Act because of domestic violence; public housing tenants give notice under the relevant regulations.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"tenants terminating under the Safe Housing Act"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-9.6","quoted_span":"If a tenant terminates the lease agreement prior to the expiration of the lease pursuant to the Safe Housing Act , (N.J.S.A. 46:8-9.6), the tenant is entitled to the return of their security deposit. Within 15 business days after the lease is terminated, the landlord shall make available the return of the tenant’s security deposit, plus any interest earned, to the tenant or the tenant’s agent.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-01","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Safe Housing Act lease termination for domestic violence victims","requirement":"A tenant who is a victim of domestic violence may end the lease without penalty by giving written notice naming the person who poses the threat and one of the listed documents; the termination takes effect 30 days after the landlord receives them, with rent pro-rated.","key_value":"30 days' notice; termination without penalty","coverage_conditions":"Tenants of residential rentals, or their children, who face an imminent threat of serious physical harm; not transient or seasonal rentals.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"residential rentals; excludes transient or seasonal rentals"},"exemptions":"Transient or seasonal rentals.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-9.4","quoted_span":"Lease Termination due to domestic violence shall take effect on the thirtieth day following receipt by the landlord of one of the documents listed above and a written notice from the tenant that they intend to vacate the premises and terminate the lease. The rent shall be pro -rated up to the time of the lease termination (N.J.S.A. 46:8-9.4 et seq.).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-01","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Lease termination for disabling illness, accident or death","requirement":"A tenant may break a lease of one or more years because of a disabling illness or accident causing loss of income, or the death of the tenant or spouse, by giving 40 days' written notice on the prescribed form and vacating five working days before the 40th day.","key_value":"40 days' written notice","coverage_conditions":"Tenants with leases of one or more years who suffer a disabling illness or accident causing loss of income, or the death of the tenant or the tenant's spouse.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"residential leases of one or more years"},"exemptions":"Leases that specifically provide otherwise.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-9.2","quoted_span":"under the Lease Termination Due to Disabling Illness, Accident or Death Law, a tenant may break their lease, under certain conditions. A 40-day written notice of lease termination is required in each instance. The tenant must vacate and return possession of the property to the landlord at least five working days prior to the 40 th day following the landlord’s receipt of the notice to terminate.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
